  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "src-01-square-cube-root-basics",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Square and Cube Root Basics",
    "description": "See square and cube roots as reverse operations, then use product, quotient, fraction and decimal root rules mentally.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with the reverse-operation meaning of roots, then build through cube roots, exact fraction roots, product and quotient laws, decimal roots, an inside-out nested radical, and a capstone that combines both root laws. Every rule is earned from a number example and checked by squaring or cubing; each idea gets fresh parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach square root as the number whose square gives the radicand and cube root as the number whose cube gives it. Start with small perfect squares and cubes and make checking by squaring or cubing part of the habit. Teach the chapter laws √(xy)=√x·√y and √(x/y)=√x/√y through friendly products and quotients, then use them on exact fractions and terminating decimals such as 0.0009. Include inside-out evaluation of a short nested-root expression and a capstone that combines product and quotient rules in one radical. Keep all roots exact and positive as used in the chapter. Do not teach prime-factor extraction, long division, surd rationalisation or perfect-square repair here.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 27"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make roots feel like something you already know.\n\nWhen 13 × 13 makes 169, √169 simply asks us to run that fact backwards. Cube roots do the same job with three equal factors.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A square is made from two equal factors.\n\n13 × 13 = 169\n\nSo √169 asks: which positive number was used twice?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The answer is 13.\n\n√169 = 13\n\nCheck by going forward again:\n13² = 169\n\nA square root undoes a square; it does not mean ‘divide by 2’.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
